{
  "dom_id": "step-q159719",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{(7y-6x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$",
  "worked_solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y-2x)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-6x)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot (-6)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14y+12x+18y-12x}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{(7y-6x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=4y.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
